diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — PC15 (172.18.51.15) being unable to reach PC16 (172.18.51.16)
on the Location B subnet — does not exist in the live lab. Direct investigation
across every relevant component produced clean results at every level of the stack.

At the endpoint level, both PC15 and PC16 have their eth0 interfaces in LOWER_UP /
UP state with correct /24 addresses on 172.18.51.0/24. At the L2 level, switch10's
Linux bridge (br0) shows all member ports — including eth1 (PC15) and eth2 (PC16)
and the uplink eth0 to mswitch2 — in the forwarding state with no STP-blocked ports
or admin-down interfaces. ARP resolution is healthy: PC15's ARP cache holds a fresh
entry mapping 172.18.51.16 to PC16's real MAC (ae:24:61:03:c2:c7). A live 5-packet
ICMP ping from PC15 to PC16 completed with 5/5 packets received, 0% loss, and
sub-millisecond round-trip times (~0.5 ms steady-state).

The ticket premise — that the access switch is dropping frames between the two ports
or that PC16's port is down — is factually incorrect. Both endpoints are reachable,
the switch is forwarding normally, and the subnet is fully operational. No corrective
action is required. The helpdesk ticket should be closed as a false alarm; the user
should be asked to re-test from their actual machine and confirm which specific
application or address failed, as the issue is not reproducible at the network layer.
=== END ===
